
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:search@

%9Parameters:%9

    -all:       Searches the logs of every network and target.
    -network:   Searches the logs of every target of this network (its tag).
    -target:    Searches the log of this channel or nick instead of the
                active window's (on the active network, on the one
                -network names, or with -all on every network).
    -max:       Shows at most this many hits, the newest; 50 by default,
                at most 1000 (a larger number is taken as 1000).
    -regexp:    The text is a regular expression.
    -case:      Matches case-sensitively.
    -stop:      Stops the search that is running.

    The text to search for. Without -all, -network or -target the
    channel or query of the active window is searched.

%9Description:%9

    Searches the autolog files for a text and shows the matching lines,
    oldest first, each with its log (the target, with the network
    before it when several are searched) and the date of its day.

    The files are the ones autolog_path names: its $tag and $0 are filled
    in for the scope, every other variable and every strftime() code
    matches anything, and '~' is your home directory. Only those files are
    read: not /LOG files, not /WINDOW LOG files, not the scrollback (see
    /LASTLOG), and not compressed (.gz) or rotated files under another
    name. Symbolic links below the log directory are not followed.

    The search runs in a separate process, so erssi goes on while it
    reads; the results are printed when it is done, in the window it was
    started in. One search runs at a time. Files are read newest first;
    a search reads at most search_max_size bytes in all (of a log that
    does not fit, its last part) and at most 5000 files. The summary line
    says when one of these limits stopped it. A search still running
    after 120 seconds is ended and says it failed (and why), without hits. Lines
    longer than 400 characters are shortened.

    /SEARCH is not available in capability mode (/CAPSICUM ENTER).

%9Settings:%9

    search_max_size = 512M
        The most a search reads, over all its files.

%9Examples:%9

    /SEARCH holiday
    /SEARCH -max 10 -target bob see you
    /SEARCH -network IRCnet -regexp holiday|vacation
    /SEARCH -all -case README
    /SEARCH -stop

%9See also:%9 LASTLOG, LOG
